binet colleagues who with my  colleagues in the Conservative Party  actually who have voted to rule out no  deal in my cabinet we will be voting to  keep no deal on the table that will be  government policy cabinet policy and I  would expect all colleagues to follow  the line on that because that will  strengthen our hand in the negotiations  [Applause]  good evening David sidwich policing  crime Commissioner candidate for Dorset  thank you will you give more core  funding to the police so that the  manpower reflects the demand and allows  them to address not only serious crime  but the distressing antisocial behavior  or if you like quality of life crime  which affects people more on a day to  day basis well first of all very best of  luck to you in the PCC elections and  thank you for the question  I was part of the government that came  into power the cabinet that came in in  May 2010 and we were facing the biggest  financial crisis that we'd had in  peacetime and we made some decisions to  put the economy back on its feet and  what Jeremy Corbyn likes to label  austerity they were very very difficult  cuts in spending and I think we were  right to do that because the result of  that is that the economy has grown jobs  have been created I was able to secure  that big increase in funding for the NHS  but I think it's true also that in one  or two areas we can see with the benefit  of high  site that some of those cuts went a bit  too far and the two areas that I would  single out are the social care system  where I do think that councils are  finding it very hard to deliver the  standard of care that we as  conservatives would expect and also  police numbers where there's a delayed  impact so crime continues to fall for a  while when you cut police numbers but  after a while it catches up with you so  those are two areas without making an  explicit spending pledge that I would  commit to look at as Prime Minister  stopped and searched expanding stopping  search committed to yes I agree with  Boris on that I agree with Sajid Javid  on that stop and search is a very  important power for the police of course  it has to be used sensitively but they  do have to have the ability to stop  someone that they suspect might be  carrying knives committing a crime  within the law and so I support them  being able to do what they need to do ok  the next question comes from Jack either  Jack Bolton or Jackie's from Bolton  Winchester actually  good evening I'd like to ask you if you  yourself could make the difficult  decision that Jeremy Corbyn cannot  namely would you use our nuclear  deterrent in extreme circumstances well  Jack from Winchester my my dad was in  the Navy  and in his last job in the Navy he  actually had responsibility for the  nuclear deterrent and this was something  that we thought about very hard in our  family and if you have a deterrent that  you say you're never going to use it's  not a deterrent and that is why it would  be completely wrong for any Prime  Minister to say that they wouldn't ever  use a nuclear deterrent the lesson from  the success of the nuclear deterrent is  that you have to be strong to be safe  and that is what we did in the Cold War  and I think we need to recognize now  that the world is becoming again  increasingly unstable when you look at  what's happening in the Middle East when  you look at what's happening in Russia  when you look at a China that is  becoming more autocratic and it's been  Britain's historic role to be one of the  very few countries that doesn't just  speak out for democratic values but also  understands you've got to have hard  power behind that to have credibility  and that at the point of Breck's in the  start of this moment in our exciting new  moment our history I want us to do that  now too  [Applause]  good evening Dorsett the National Health  Service has taken on the status of a  national religion and is often said to  be beyond sensible management and reform  